CS 214 Project IV - Simple Chat Server
------------------------------------------
Partners:
  [Darren Chuang]   [dlc286]
  [Numair Hasan] [Mnh83]

Build:
  make

Run:
  ./chatd <port>

Example:
  ./chatd 9000

Design
---------------------
chatd uses one thread per connected client (pthreads).
A global linked list (head) stores all connected account_user structs.
All accesses to the list are protected by clients_mutex.

Message parsing:
  parse_message() reads the header byte-by-byte to extract the
  message type and body length, then reads exactly body_len bytes
  for the body.  This avoids deadlock by never reading past the
  declared length.  The body is split on '|' into fields[] in-place.

Each message type has its own handler:
  handle_nam  - validate and register a screen name
  handle_set  - update status, broadcast change
  handle_msg  - forward to one user or broadcast to #all
  handle_who  - respond with user or room info

Testing:
------------------------
1. BASIC CONNECTION AND NAM
   - Connect with netcat:  nc localhost 9000
   - Send:  1|NAM|4|Bob|
   - Expect welcome MSG back
   - Send same name from a second terminal
   - Expect ERR code 1 (name in use)

2. ILLEGAL NAME CHARACTERS
   - Send:  1|NAM|5|Bo b!|   (space and exclamation mark are illegal)
   - Expect ERR code 3

3. NAME TOO LONG
   - Send a NAM with a 33-character name
   - Expect ERR code 4

4. STATUS (SET)
   - After logging in as Bob, send:  1|SET|17|Smiling politely|
   - Expect broadcast MSG:  Bob is now "Smiling politely"
   - Send WHO query for Bob from another client
   - Expect:  Bob: Smiling politely

5. WHO – SINGLE USER
   - Connect two clients (Bob and Alice)
   - Bob sends:  1|WHO|6|Alice|
   - Expect MSG from #all to Bob with Alice's status or "No status"

6. WHO – ROOM
   - Bob sends:  1|WHO|5|#all|
   - Expect a MSG listing all connected users with statuses

7. WHO – UNKNOWN USER
   - Bob sends:  1|WHO|6|Carol|  (Carol not connected)
   - Expect ERR code 2

8. MSG TO #all (BROADCAST)
   - Bob sends:  1|MSG|20||#all|Hello, world!|
   - All connected clients should receive:
       1|MSG|23|Bob|#all|Hello, world!|

9. MSG – PRIVATE
   - Bob sends:  1|MSG|28||Alice|Private message to Alice|
   - Only Alice receives the forwarded message
   - Bob does NOT receive a copy

10. MSG – UNKNOWN RECIPIENT
    - Bob sends a MSG to Carol who is not connected
    - Expect ERR code 2

11. MSG TOO LONG
    - Send a message body exceeding 80 characters
    - Expect ERR code 4

12. MSG – ILLEGAL CHARACTER
    - Include a byte below 32 in the message body
    - Expect ERR code 3

13. DISCONNECT CLEANUP
    - Connect three clients; disconnect one
    - Run WHO #all from a remaining client
    - Disconnected client should no longer appear

14. CONCURRENT CLIENTS
    - Open 5+ terminals all connected at once
    - Broadcast from one; verify all others receive it
    - This stress-tests mutex correctness

15. MALFORMED PROTOCOL (ERR 0 / fatal)
    - Send a message with wrong version number:  2|NAM|4|Bob|
    - Expect ERR code 0 and connection closed by server
    - Send a body without a trailing |
    - Expect ERR code 0 and connection closed

16. MSG BEFORE NAM
    - Try sending MSG before NAM
    - Expect ERR code 0 (must set name first)

--------------------------------------------------
HOW WE TESTED
--------------------------------------------------
Manual testing was done using netcat (nc) in multiple terminals.
We typed protocol messages by hand to verify each scenario above.
We also wrote a small shell script to automate the basic NAM +
broadcast sequence across three parallel nc sessions.
